<https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability#Repairability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Ability of a defective product or waste object to return, through one or more actions, to a condition in which it fulfils its intended use. For textile apparel, user acceptance of restored functionality and potentially changed aesthetics is contextually relevant."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability#Repairability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability#Repairability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Repairability"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability#Repairability> <https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-connector#modellingNote> "Repair may extend product use, but the outcome depends on failure type, service availability, cost, user acceptance and the effectiveness of the intervention; no deterministic inference is asserted."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-repairability#Repairability> <https://w3id.org/eudpp/textile-connector#sourceLocator> "JRC Preparatory Study, relevant section, printed pages 15-16, lines 218-222; JRC Preparatory Study, Section 9.2.3, printed page 127, lines 3355-3360; JRC Preparatory Study, Section 13.10.1.5, printed page 473, lines 11273-11279; JRC Preparatory Study, relevant section, Table 2, printed page 15; caption line 219."@en .
